README

This sample program demonstrates the use of two independent TCP/IP stacks in
one application, each bound to its own Ethernet device. It has been written for
the Compute Module 5, which provides one on-board Ethernet port, extended with
a second port using a USB Ethernet device, but works on any Raspberry Pi model
with two available net devices.

Required hardware:

	* A Raspberry Pi with an on-board Ethernet controller (e.g. CM5)
	* A USB Ethernet device with a CDC Ethernet (ECM) interface, for
	  instance based on a Realtek RTL815x chip. On the Raspberry Pi 5 it
	  has to be attached to a root port directly, because USB 3 hubs are
	  not supported.
	* Both ports connected to a network with a DHCP server

The sample creates two CNetSubSystem instances. The first one is bound to net
device 0, the second one to net device 1. The on-board Ethernet device is
initialized before the USB host controller (see CKernel::Initialize()), so that
it is registered as net device 0 and the USB Ethernet device becomes net device
1. Without this explicit call the USB device would be registered first, because
USB devices are detected, while the USB host controller is initialized.

Both stacks are initialized with the parameter FALSE, so that neither of them
waits for its network to come up. This way a port with a down link does not
block the other one.

Each stack requests its own DHCP lease and has its own IP configuration,
routing and sockets. Every five seconds the MAC address and the currently
assigned IP address of both stacks is written to the log. Both IP addresses can
be pinged from another host on the network at the same time. When you unplug
one of the network cables, the other stack continues to work.

Please note that a socket always belongs to exactly one CNetSubSystem instance,
which is specified when the socket is created. There is no routing between the
two stacks.
